parsed.answer_markdown:
The models that support batching are those used in the examples provided, such as `mistral-small-latest`, though the documentation does not explicitly list all supported models for batching. The examples explicitly mention `mistral-small-latest` being used for batch processing [3].

```python
created_job = client.batch.jobs.create(
    input_files=[batch_data.id],
    model="mistral-small-latest",
    endpoint="/v1/chat/completions",
    metadata={"job_type": "testing"}
)
```

```typescript
const createdJob = await client.batch.jobs.create({
    inputFiles: [batchData.id],
    model: "mistral-small-latest",
    endpoint: "/v1/chat/completions",
    metadata: {jobType: "testing"}
});
```

```bash
curl --location "https://api.mistral.ai/v1/batch/jobs" \
--header "Authorization: Bearer $MISTRAL_API_KEY" \
--header "Content-Type: application/json" \
--header "Accept: application/json" \
--data '{
    "model": "mistral-small-latest",
    "input_files": [
        "<uuid>"
    ],
    "endpoint": "/v1/chat/completions",
    "metadata": {
        "job_type": "testing"
    }
}'
```

For inline batching, another example uses `mistral-small-latest` [3].

```python
created_job = client.batch.jobs.create(
    requests=inline_batch_data,
    model="mistral-small-latest",
    endpoint="/v1/chat/completions",
    metadata={"job_type": "testing"}
)
```

The documentation does not provide a comprehensive list of all models that support batching, so the information is limited to the examples given.

